    a("Reading, `:CALCULATED`: from three digits up, primes and composites are both nilpotent about half the time (over 2…100,000: primes 49.1 %, composites 50.0 %); the excess in the 16 sedenion basis primes (11 of 16) and the 15 Moonshine primes (12 of 15) is the two-digit window (primes 17/21, composites 29/69, composites ending 1, 3, 7, 9 only 4/15; for that one 90-number table the one-sided hypergeometric P is 0.0016, a window chosen after looking, so a look-elsewhere number). Against random primes up to 59, five of five is not rare (P = 0.127, 1 in 8, against 1 in 32 for random words); 12 of 15 Moonshine primes in a pool of 20 has P(≥ 12) = 0.36. The 16 sedenion basis primes are every prime up to 53, so their own control is vacuous. Why three digits wash the effect out is open; the author's reading, that three digits carry the three-part structure (inclination, declination, distance; Add:Scale:Sign), is consistent with the data and untested. Post-Hoc: Mauduit and Rivat (2010) find the binary digit sum of primes equidistributed; the address here is not the prime, so that is a parallel, not a proof. The same correction reaches `fermat_monster_engine.t32_nilpotency_check`, which now reports the baseline and the control.\n")
