    # ── engine 5
    a("## 8. Engine 5 · `box_kite` — the 42 assessors, the seven box-kites, the associator and the labelled group `ESTABLISHED` (de Marrais) · `OURS` (the enumerations)\n")
    a(f"Module [`modules/box_kite/`]({GH}modules/box_kite/) (0.4): `maths.py` (the Cayley–Dickson table by doubling and everything derived from it), `tools.py` (`BoxKiteModule`), tests `tests/test_box_kite.py` (9), notebook `notebooks/engines/15_box_kite.ipynb`. The zero-divisors of the sedenions (the 16-dimensional Cayley–Dickson algebra) come in 42 planes called assessors, which fall into seven octahedral families, the box-kites (de Marrais 2000, `ESTABLISHED`; first named for these objects there). The engine derives all of it from the multiplication table: nothing is read from the literature, which is used as a check. Its distinguishing rule is that e₀ lies outside the geometry it generates.\n")
    a("### 8.1 The table, the associator and the census — Convention B\n")
    a("The product is built by doubling: a pair (a, b) of elements of the lower algebra multiplies as (a, b)(c, d) = (ac − d̄b, da + b c̄). Listing 14 is the doubling written as a function on indices (e_i · e_j = ± e_k), then the associator [e_i, e_j, e_k] = (e_i e_j) e_k − e_i (e_j e_k) the engine calls the curvature, then its census over all 16³ triples.\n")
    bk_hdr = "from typing import Dict, Tuple"
    a(listing("the sedenion table by doubling, the associator, the census", "modules/box_kite/maths.py: cd_multiplication_table, basis_mul, is_zero, associator, associator_census",
              [BKM.cd_multiplication_table, BKM.basis_mul, BKM.is_zero, BKM.associator, BKM.associator_census], bk_hdr,
              "_SED_TABLE, SEDENION_DIM = cd_multiplication_table(4)\nprint(basis_mul(1, 2), basis_mul(2, 1))\nprint(basis_mul(1, 9), basis_mul(9, 1))\nprint(associator_census())"))
    a("`:CALCULATED`: 1848 of the 4096 basis triples do not associate, and every one of them has norm exactly 2. The octonion block (all three indices below 8) is not flat: 168 of its 512 triples bend (the ordered triples of distinct imaginary units that do not lie on a Fano line); an earlier plan for the pictures called it flat, which the census does not support (ledger X-69). `tests/test_box_kite.py` checks the table against an independent recursive Cayley–Dickson product.\n")
    a("### 8.2 The counts, derived — Convention D\n")
    a(transcript("the counts the literature states, computed", "from ValaQuenta.modules.box_kite import maths as bk\nr = bk.verify_counts()\nprint({k: r[k] for k in ('assessors', 'diagonals', 'unit_points', 'ordered_zd_pairs', 'kills_per_diagonal', 'box_kites')})\nprint('strut of (3, 2) =', bk.strut(3, 2), '; kite 1 =', bk.box_kites()[1])"))
    a("42 assessors (49 planes span(e_a, e_(b+8)) with a, b in 1…7, minus the 7 with a = b), 84 diagonals, 168 unit points, 336 ordered pairs of diagonals whose product vanishes, each diagonal annihilating exactly four others, and seven box-kites keyed by the strut a XOR b, each of six assessors. Strut 1 is de Marrais's Box-Kite I.\n")
    a("### 8.3 The octahedron and its spectrum — Convention D\n")
    a(transcript("one box-kite is an octahedron", "from ValaQuenta.modules.box_kite import maths as bk\ng = bk.box_kite_graph(1)\nprint(g['vertices'])\nprint(len(g['edges']), 'edges; 4-regular:', g['is_4_regular'], '; octahedron:', g['is_octahedron'])\nprint(g['non_edges'])\nprint([round(x, 6) for x in bk.chart_spectrum(1)])"))
    a("The six assessors are the vertices, an edge joins two whose diagonals annihilate each other, and the three non-edges are the reversal pairs (a, b), (b, a): K₂,₂,₂, the octahedron. Its Laplacian spectrum {0, 4, 4, 4, 6, 6} is the same for all seven struts. The glued graph of all 42 has 84 edges and none between two kites, so its spectrum is the same seven times: how the kites are joined into one geometry is the engine's stated open item, and the pictures do not fake it.\n")
    a("### 8.4 The labelled group — Convention A for the function (long), D for the result\n")
    a("`labelled_automorphisms` (about 60 lines; not excerpted) tries every permutation of the seven imaginary units (5040), keeps the 168 that preserve the seven Fano lines, tries the 128 sign patterns on each, and keeps the signed permutations that preserve the table. `labelled_group_is_split` lifts a generating pair of the 168 in all 64 ways and generates the subgroup each spans.\n")
    a(transcript("the group of the labelled octonions", "from ValaQuenta.modules.box_kite import maths as bk\nr = bk.labelled_automorphisms()\nprint(r['signed_automorphisms'], '=', r['sign_kernel'], '*', r['quotient_order'])\nprint('extends to the sedenions:', r['extend_to_sedenions'], '; orbits on the', r['unit_points'], 'unit points:', r['orbits_on_unit_points'], '; stabiliser', r['point_stabiliser'])\ns = bk.labelled_group_is_split()\nprint(s['lift_pairs_tried'], 'lift pairs; subgroup orders', s['subgroup_orders'], '; split:', s['split'])"))
    a("`:CALCULATED`: the group is of order 1344 = 8 · 168; PSL(2,7) is its quotient, not the group, and it acts transitively on the 168 unit zero-divisor points with a stabiliser of order 8, so 168 is 1344 / 8 and not a regular PSL(2,7) action. The extension 2³·PSL(2,7) is non-split. Before this draft the engine stated that PSL(2,7) was the finite subgroup of G₂ preserving the labelling and `psl27_order()` returned the constant 168; both are replaced by the enumeration (ledger X-68, X-69).\n")
