    a("## 10. Engine 7 · `bracketing_firing_order` — bracketing, firing order and set membership, on a 16-component vector `ESTABLISHED` (counts) · `OURS` (the four orderings, the plan layer)\n")
    a(f"Module [`modules/bracketing_firing_order/`]({GH}modules/bracketing_firing_order/) (0.2): `maths.py` (the three tools and the plan layer), `tools.py` (`BracketingFiringOrderModule`, 9 equations), tests `tests/test_bracketing_firing_order.py` (17; the engine had none before this pass). Three general tools, kept apart: **bracketing** (how many ways to group n things), **firing order** (how many ways to sequence them) and **set membership** (has a position been visited; is an operation in an object's own set). Version 0.2 adds the plan layer: brackets that carry firing orders, run on 16-component vectors in exact `Fraction` arithmetic, with the input and the output of every plan kept side by side. Ĥ_RB has the same shape: one 16-component σ_RB in, one 16-component sedenion out.\n")
    a("### 10.1 The sedenion product and the counts — Convention B\n")
    a("The element reading of a plan multiplies sedenions, so the module carries its own exact Cayley–Dickson product (module independence: no import from another engine). Listing 16 is that product on any power-of-two length; Listing 17 is the exact number of plans of each of the four kinds.\n")
    a(listing("the exact Cayley–Dickson product", "modules/bracketing_firing_order/maths.py: sedenion_mul (with its conjugate)", [BFM._conj, BFM.sedenion_mul],
              "from fractions import Fraction as F\nfrom typing import Any, Sequence, Tuple\n\nVec = Tuple[F, ...]",
              "e = lambda k: [int(i == k) for i in range(16)]\nprint([int(v) for v in sedenion_mul(e(1), e(2))])\nprint([int(v) for v in sedenion_mul(e(2), e(1))])"))
    a(listing("the number of plans of each kind", "modules/bracketing_firing_order/maths.py: mode_counts", [BFM.mode_counts],
              "import math\nfrom typing import Dict",
              "print(mode_counts(3))\nprint(mode_counts(4))\nprint(mode_counts(16))"))
    a("`:CALCULATED`: for n items, A bracketing is Catalan(n−1) plans, B firing order n!, C firing order of brackets Catalan(n−1)·2ⁿ⁻¹, and D bracketing of firing orders n!·Catalan(n−1) = (2n−2)!/(n−1)!. D is every ordered binary product of the n items, so A, B and C are contained in it (tested by value on random 16-vectors). The firing sequences the kinds can produce differ: A one, B and D all n!, C only the separable permutations (6 of 6, 22 of 24, 90 of 120 for n = 3, 4, 5).\n")
    a("### 10.2 The four orderings on a 16-component vector — Convention D\n")
    a(transcript("how many plans, and how many different outputs", "from ValaQuenta.modules.bracketing_firing_order import maths as bf\nfrom ValaQuenta.modules.bracketing_firing_order.tools import _A, _B, _C, _X\nel = bf.compare_modes([_A, _B, _C])\nop = bf.compare_modes([_A, _B, _C], 'operator', _X)\nprint({m: (el[m]['plans'], el[m]['distinct_outputs']) for m in 'ABCD'})\nprint({m: (op[m]['plans'], op[m]['distinct_outputs']) for m in 'ABCD'})\nprint([bf.reachable_orders([bf.Leaf(c) for c in 'ABCD'], m) for m in 'ABCD'])"))
    a("Two readings of every plan. In the **operator** reading each item is its left-multiplication matrix and a bracket composes them, first fired applied first; matrices associate, so the bracketing does nothing and only the firing order changes the output (above: one output for A, one per reachable firing order for the others). In the **element** reading a bracket is the sedenion product of its fired children, folded left to right; the sedenion product is not associative (252 of the 455 triples of distinct imaginary basis units do not associate), so the bracketing changes the output as well: for generic sedenions every plan gives its own output.\n")
    a("### 10.3 Watching the vector evolve — Convention D\n")
    a(transcript("the input, the vector after each item fires, the output", "from ValaQuenta.modules.bracketing_firing_order import maths as bf\nfrom ValaQuenta.modules.bracketing_firing_order.tools import _A, _B, _C, _X\nleaves = {n: bf.Leaf.of(n, v) for n, v in zip('ABC', (_A, _B, _C))}\nplan = bf.parse_plan('((A B | 2 1) C | 2 1)', leaves)\nsteps = bf.evolve_operator(plan, _X)\nprint(bf.notation(plan), 'fires', bf.fire_sequence(plan))\nfor name, v in steps:\n    print(name.ljust(6), [int(x) for x in v][:6], '...')\nprint([s[0] for s in bf.evolve_value(plan)])"))
    a("`evolve_operator` returns the input and the vector after each item fires; `evolve_value` returns every product of the element reading in the order it is computed. The last state is the output and the first the input; the gallery draws all of them as 16 bars on the axes e₀…e₁₅ so the change can be watched. Ĥ_RB lift B is the one-item plan x ↦ L_C x, and a test checks that its output is the product C·σ_RB.\n")
